<ul>
    <li>Along the path of <strong>Reference Wiki → feature experiment → validation → company PoC → stabilization → organizational rollout</strong>, nothing unvalidated is introduced to the organization directly.</li>
    <li>Documents are operated <strong>HTML-first</strong>, making use of tables · dashboards · SVG diagrams · <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> · status badges · warning boxes · architecture flows · DB structure visualizations.</li>
    <li>The increase in AI tokens caused by HTML is minimized by <strong>Knowledge Index based retrieval</strong>, which does not read whole documents. This also aligns with the <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pattern officially recommended by Anthropic</a>, which turns organizational knowledge into AI context through the CLAUDE.md hierarchy and MCP integration.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The AI consumption layer — three doors machines read</h3>
<p>The promise that "AI uses organizational knowledge as context" also already exists in physical form in the Reference Wiki (<a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">How AI Reads This Wiki</a>). External AI comes in through three doors: ① <code>llms.txt</code> — exposes the cover page of the whole knowledge (what is where) following the llmstxt.org convention → ② <code>knowledge-graph.json</code> — a self-contained file that delivers the entire graph (nodes · related links · typed relations · concepts · each document's URL) in <strong>a single fetch</strong> → ③ fetching only the individual documents that are needed. It is a structure in which AI follows relations and reads only as much as it needs, without embedding the whole corpus or crawling every time; these files are generated automatically from the index, and the <code>--check</code> gate prevents them from being deployed stale. Connecting the company platform to Claude Code and AI agents is a matter of moving this same contract onto intranet URLs.</p>

<h3>Security and access structure</h3>
<p>Company development knowledge may include internal architecture, customer project information, DB structures, incident analyses, security settings, and authentication-related information. Knowledge grades are therefore separated, and repositories and access are separated as well.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>PUBLIC</strong> — general knowledge readable by all employees</li>
    <li><strong>INTERNAL</strong> — technical knowledge within the scope of the development organization</li>
    <li><strong>CONFIDENTIAL</strong> — knowledge containing customer/project sensitive information</li>
    <li><strong>RESTRICTED</strong> — security knowledge accessible only to restricted organizations</li>
</ul>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">Do not mix it all into one public wiki</span>
    The recommended structure separates repositories and access into <strong>General Wiki (all employees) · Engineering Wiki (development organization) · Restricted Knowledge (restricted organizations)</strong>. All security grades are not mixed into a single public wiki, and employees search and read only within the scope of their own permissions.
</div>

<h3>Added Value and Use Scenarios — Beyond the Development Organization</h3>
<p>The previous parts dealt with the perspective inside the development organization — how knowledge is created and managed. This part deals with <strong>how many faces a single Knowledge State, once accumulated, can be consumed in</strong>. The source of added value is not writing more documents, but <strong>adding one more view per audience</strong> on the repository that has already been accumulated — accumulate once, and employees, partners, customers, and AI each pull the same knowledge within their own scope.</p>

<h4>Automatic API specification updates — the first derivative product</h4>
<p>The most immediate added value is that <strong>the API specification stays alive together with the code</strong>. The chronic problem of manual specifications is well known — updates fall behind after the first draft, and a specification that diverges from the code misleads integration developers and customers. On this platform, the API specification is not a separate deliverable but a <strong>natural output of the knowledge pipeline</strong>:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Extraction</strong> — endpoints · parameters · response structures · error codes are read out of merged Controller · Mapper · Config changes. Because they are facts confirmed directly in the code, most of them are <strong>FACT grade</strong> — the knowledge with the highest chance of automatic publication in the <a href="#!dzp-knowledge">trust level system</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Update</strong> — when an API changes, the same merge trigger updates the specification document. The dual management of "specification here, code there" structurally disappears.</li>
    <li><strong>Search</strong> — because API names and error codes are carried in the <code>identifiers</code> extension field, an integration developer reaches the relevant specification and policy document directly from a single error code.</li>
    <li><strong>History</strong> — when an interface policy changes, the old specification is linked as superseded via <code>supersedes</code>, preserving both "the current spec" and "why it changed."</li>
</ul>

<h4>One repository, a view per audience</h4>
<p>The four security grades are an access control mechanism and at the same time a <strong>publication channel selector</strong>. From the same repository, read-only views per audience are created simply by varying the grade filter — no repository is cloned and no document is copied over.</p>

