<h4>Prior knowledge for AI coding — where the virtuous cycle closes</h4>
<p>The most strategic use is <strong>using the knowledge graph as prior knowledge for AI coding</strong>. Today, a developer re-explains the domain background to an AI tool such as Claude Code every single time — "the sales order status transitions like this, the credit check happens at that point…" (the cost of repeatedly re-explaining context that <a href="#!dzp-overview">Why Now</a> pointed out). On this platform, AI reads the knowledge graph first, <strong>before starting the work</strong>:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Entry</strong> — it grasps the overall terrain of the knowledge through llms.txt, and secures nodes · relations · concepts · URLs with <strong>a single fetch</strong> of knowledge-graph.json.</li>
    <li><strong>Selection</strong> — it reaches the related policy documents via the <code>identifiers</code> (table, rule ID, API name) it meets in the source it is working on, and expands only as far as needed by following typed relations — background through prerequisites, "the policy valid now" through supersedes, the reasons behind design decisions through the DECISION grade.</li>
    <li><strong>Work</strong> — it reads and writes code <strong>already knowing</strong> the domain rules, status transitions, past incidents, and design intent. This procedure can be fixed as a project convention through CLAUDE.md and MCP integration.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is the substance of the fourth box ("Next development — Claude Code · Agent") in the virtuous cycle diagram of the <a href="#!dzp-overview">overview</a> — development results become knowledge, and that knowledge is <strong>injected as prior knowledge into the AI of the next development</strong>, raising the quality of development results again. It is a structure in which, the more knowledge accumulates, the more the accuracy of AI coding rises at organizational scale like compound interest, and the Reference Wiki's <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">traversal contract</a> has already demonstrated this mode of consumption at small scale.</p>

<h4>Reaching non-development roles too — trust grades widen the readership</h4>
<p>Development knowledge does not belong to developers alone. <strong>Technical support and CS</strong> search incident cases and troubleshooting to raise the quality of first-line response, <strong>sales and PMs</strong> raise the accuracy of customer conversations with product feature, policy, and terminology knowledge, and <strong>new joiners</strong> use the organization's knowledge map as an onboarding path regardless of their role. The reason it is safe even when non-development readers cannot read code is that a <a href="#!dzp-knowledge">trust grade</a> is attached to every document — FACT (facts confirmed in the code) is distinguished from INFERRED (inference) and DECISION (decision), so readers do not misunderstand how certain the knowledge is.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Key point</span>
    The added value of this platform does not come from creating a new documentation channel for every department. <strong>It comes from adding views with grade filters on top of a Knowledge State accumulated once</strong> — API specifications, partner guides, the customer documentation portal, and prior knowledge for AI coding are all different cross-sections of the same repository.
</div>

<h3>Rollout Roadmap — 4 Phases and the First PoC</h3>
<p>Nothing unvalidated is introduced to the organization. Starting from pre-validation, the steps proceed in the order of team PoC, center-wide expansion, and company-wide rollout.</p>

<h4>Four-phase rollout</h4>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Phase 1 · Porting the Reference Wiki engine</strong> — we do not start from a blank page: <strong>install wiki-plugin (v0.21.0)</strong> in the company repository to bootstrap the registration, index, and validation system as it is (the wiki-init → add-doc → validate skills of <a href="#!kgs-plugin">the plugin packaging</a>), manually curate SCM domain knowledge through Claude Code integration, and validate Knowledge Index search, HTML accumulation, and the AI Curator Prototype. First target: <strong>OmniEsol ERP · SCM domain</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Phase 2 · Company PoC (SCM구축개발1Unit)</strong> — company Wiki Repository, GitLab Project Registry, Change Collector, SHA incremental processing, AI Knowledge Curator, introduction of extension fields (identifiers · sources · trust), Read-Only Portal. First source: the OmniEsol ERP SCM GitLab Repository</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 3 · Expansion to the SCM Development Center</strong> — multiple projects, Product/Domain classification, knowledge links across projects (applying typed relations across projects), Knowledge Lifecycle (operating supersedes), security grades, quality KPIs</li>
    <li><strong>Phase 4 · Company-wide expansion</strong> — in-house SSO, company-wide search, per-product Knowledge Governance, AI Agent integration (intranet llms.txt and graph JSON contracts), formalization as the in-house development platform</li>
</ul>

<h4>First PoC — OmniEsol ERP (SCM domain)</h4>
<p>ERP is <strong>the domain with the highest density of business rules</strong>. The business rules of sales, sales orders, purchasing, and production; the document status transition system (sales order → shipment → revenue); credit and available-to-promise (ATP) check policies; inter-process integration; DB table structures and column semantics; incident cases; policy change history — <strong>elements with high value for turning into knowledge already exist</strong>, and the search and accumulation effects can be validated immediately with identifiers such as those below (for the entry structure, see the schema example in <a href="#!dzp-knowledge">Knowledge Management</a>).</p>
<pre><code>SD_SO_HDR   SD_SO_ITEM   SO_STS_CD
CREDIT_CHK_YN   NR_SD_SO_COMPLETED   createSalesOrder</code></pre>

<h4>Validation KPIs — four axes, measured by gates</h4>
<p>A KPI is not a declaration; <strong>it becomes a target only when a measurement method is attached</strong>. A good part of the four axes are values the Reference Wiki is already measuring with CI gates, so the company PoC does not need to invent the measuring instruments anew.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Knowledge generation quality</strong> — accuracy of automatic generation, success rate of updating existing documents, duplicate document creation rate, rate of wrong inferences. <em>Measurement</em>: validator ERROR 0 (broken references, duplicate IDs, unregistered documents) + index drift 0 (<code>--check</code>) + orphan documents 0 (graph validation) — all judged automatically on every commit. Inference errors are sample-inspected per <code>trust</code> grade.</li>
    <li><strong>Search quality</strong> — identifier search success rate (target direction: reaching the policy document from a single identifier met in the source ≥90%), accuracy of related document recommendations, time to reach the desired information.</li>
    <li><strong>Development productivity</strong> — fewer repeated questions, less re-analysis of past incidents, reduced learning time for new developers (the 3–9 month onboarding and 8 hours per week loss statistics of <a href="#!dzp-overview">Why Now</a> are the baseline), less context explanation for AI tools.</li>
    <li><strong>Operational quality</strong> — <strong>knowledge reflection rate against merges (target direction ≥90%)</strong>: "was knowledge reflection accompanied in merges with substantive changes" is measured continuously as the pass rate of the diff-based accompaniment gate (the integrity gate of the <a href="#!dzp-core">architecture</a>). Number of missed changes (aggregated by the sync-state correction batch), superseded processing rate, distribution of relation types (whether it is skewed toward prerequisites — a non-blocking review signal is surfaced for every new document).</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Closing</span>
    This proposal is about creating a <strong>virtuous cycle</strong> (<a href="#!dzp-overview">overview</a>) without changing developers' workflow, and that knowledge engine is already running as the Reference Wiki. What remains is to connect the event stream called GitLab to the validated engine.
</div>
